NexusAI — Personal AI Operating System
Tagline: One intelligent companion for your life, learning, and
work.
Product type: AI-powered Android application
Initial market: University students, early-career professionals,
freelancers, and solo founders in Pakistan
Long-term vision: A trusted, multilingual personal AI workspace
that helps people plan, understand information, and complete everyday
digital tasks.


1. Product vision
NexusAI is a mobile-first personal AI workspace. A user can talk or type
naturally, give it documents, define goals, and ask it to help organize
work. Rather than being another generic chatbot, NexusAI connects
conversation to useful, user-approved workflows: create a task,
summarize a PDF, prepare a study plan, draft a professional message, or
review progress.

Core promise: Turn a user’s intention into a clear plan and, with
permission, a useful action.

What NexusAI is — and is not
Is: an assistant, document companion, planner, and lightweight
workflow tool.
Is not (at launch): an autonomous agent with unrestricted access
to a user’s phone, accounts, messages, or money.

Trust principle: show what will happen, request permission
before external actions, and let the user review, edit, undo, or
delete their data.

Problem to solve
People switch between chatbots, notes, calendars, PDF readers, task
apps, and career resources. They lose context and repeatedly explain
their goals. NexusAI aims to bring a small number of high-frequency
tasks into one coherent experience.

Positioning
“Your personal AI workspace — conversation that turns into organized
work.”

Do not market it as the first, only, or world’s best AI operating
system. Compete on a focused audience, excellent execution, privacy,
local relevance, and reliable workflows.


2. Target audiences
Start narrow. Do not try to serve everyone in version 1.

Primary beachhead: university students (18+)
Need help understanding lecture notes, PDFs, and course material.
Need assignment planning, revision schedules, and reminders.

Need help drafting emails, CVs, internship applications, and study
plans.
Often use Android phones and may prefer affordable pricing.

Initial validation: interview 20–30 students from different
departments and universities.

Secondary audiences
Freelancers: project briefs, client-message drafts, task
breakdowns, proposal outlines, and weekly planning.

Early-career professionals: meeting-note summaries, task lists,
document Q&A, and career planning.

Solo founders and small teams: idea validation, SOP drafts,
project plans, and recurring checklists.

Lifelong learners: explainers, learning plans, and personal
knowledge organization.

First launch geography
Begin with Pakistan, test English and Urdu/Roman Urdu usability, and
then expand based on actual retention and support capacity. Do not
assume all users want the same language or price. Measure demand before
adding localization.

User personas
Persona Main job to be done First feature to win
them

University student Understand material and PDF Q&A + study planner
stay organized

Freelancer Turn a client brief Brief-to-task workflow
into deliverables

Graduate/job seeker Prepare career CV and application
documents workspace

3. Product strategy: build a wedge, then expand

The long-term vision is broad, but the first release must be small.

MVP: the first useful product

Build only these five user journeys:

1.  Sign up and onboarding: explain value, collect only essential
    preferences, and allow guest/demo exploration if feasible.
2.  AI chat: text conversation with a clear usage limit and
    conversation history.
3.  Document assistant: upload a supported PDF, ask questions about
    it, and receive answers with page/section references where
    technically possible.
4.  Action cards: convert an AI response into a task, checklist,
    study plan, or reminder after user confirmation.
5.  Personal workspace: view, edit, complete, export, and delete
    saved tasks and plans.

Later releases

-   Voice input and spoken responses.
-   Urdu and Roman Urdu quality improvements.
-   Calendar integration, only after explicit permission and a clear
    consent screen.
-   Image understanding and OCR.
-   Personal knowledge search across user-approved documents.
-   Freelancer and founder templates.
-   Team workspace and shared projects.
-   Carefully scoped integrations with third-party services.

Explicitly out of scope for MVP

-   Sending emails or messages automatically.
-   Making purchases, payments, financial decisions, or bookings.
-   Reading all phone data or notifications.
-   Unrestricted background monitoring.
-   Training models on private user documents by default.
-   Promising perfect factual accuracy or guaranteed academic/career
    outcomes.

4. Product requirements and acceptance criteria
AI chat
- User can send text and receive a streamed or clearly loading
  response.
- Show errors and retry options without duplicating user messages.
- Show model limitations and encourage verification for high-stakes
  information.
- Provide regenerate, copy, delete, and report-feedback controls.
- Apply per-user rate limits and cost controls on the server.

Document assistant
- Clearly list supported file formats and size limits.
- Validate file type and size on both client and server.
- Parse documents securely; reject malformed or unsupported files.
- Keep document access private to the owner.
- Ground answers in the uploaded document and indicate when the answer
  is not present.
- Allow users to delete a document and its derived chunks/indexes.
- Never expose one user's document to another user.

Tasks and plans
- User can create, edit, complete, and delete an item.
- AI-generated actions are previews until the user confirms.
- Store due date/time with an explicit timezone.
- Notifications are opt-in and can be disabled.

Privacy and safety
- Plain-language privacy notice and terms before account creation.
- Ask only for data needed for a feature.
- Provide account/data deletion and document deletion workflows.
- Encrypt traffic in transit; apply appropriate encryption and access
  controls at rest.
- Do not put API secrets in the Android app.
- Do not log raw private prompts/documents unnecessarily.
- Define retention periods and deletion behavior before public launch.
- Do not use user content for model training unless there is a
  separate, informed opt-in and a lawful basis.
- Add abuse prevention, prompt-injection defenses for retrieved
  documents, and safeguards for harmful or high-stakes requests.

5. Recommended technology architecture
Choose boring, maintainable technology. The product's differentiation is
user experience and workflows, not the number of frameworks.
Android
- Language: Kotlin
- UI: Jetpack Compose + Material 3
- Architecture: MVVM or a clean, modular presentation/domain/data
  structure
- Async work: Kotlin Coroutines and Flow
- Networking: Retrofit or Ktor client
- Local storage: Room for appropriate cached/offline data;
  DataStore for preferences
- Navigation: Navigation Compose
- Dependency injection: Hilt or a simpler manual approach for a
  small MVP
- Notifications: Firebase Cloud Messaging where appropriate
- Crash reporting/analytics: privacy-conscious, consent-aware
  tools
Backend
- Language/API: Python + FastAPI
- Validation: Pydantic
- Database: PostgreSQL for users, plans, tasks, subscriptions, and
  audit metadata
- Vector search: add pgvector or a managed vector store only when
  document retrieval needs it
- File storage: private object storage with signed, short-lived
  access URLs
- Background jobs: add a queue/worker for document processing when
  synchronous requests become unreliable
- Authentication: Firebase Authentication or another established
  identity provider; verify tokens on the backend
- Payments: use a compliant payment provider for each target
  market; verify store billing requirements before selling digital
  features inside the Android app

AI layer
Create a provider-independent service interface so the app is not locked
to one model vendor.
- Start with one capable hosted LLM API.
- Use a smaller/cheaper model for simple classification or formatting
  only after quality testing.
- Add retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) for document Q&A.
- Use embeddings only for search/retrieval needs.
- Add prompt templates, output schemas, timeouts, retries, rate
  limits, and token budgets.
- Evaluate responses against a fixed test set before changing models.
- Record model/version and cost metadata, not unnecessary sensitive
  content.
- Never trust instructions found inside uploaded documents; treat them
  as untrusted content.
High-level architecture

Android app (Kotlin + Compose)
        |
        | HTTPS + authenticated requests
        v
FastAPI backend
        |
        +--> Authentication/token verification
        +--> User, task, plan and subscription services
        +--> AI orchestration / provider adapter
        +--> Document ingestion and retrieval
        +--> Rate limits, quotas, audit events
        |
        +--> PostgreSQL (+ pgvector when needed)
        +--> Private object storage
        +--> Background worker (later)
        +--> LLM provider API

Important: The Android client must call your backend for model
requests. Keep provider API keys and privileged credentials on the
server, never in APK source, BuildConfig, remote config, or Git.

